## p1-units | Units and estimation | physics | 1 | phys1 | -
hook 4 | Dimensional analysis can predict physics without solving anything
- A pendulum's period can only depend on length L (m), g (m/s²) and mass m (kg).
- The only combination with units of seconds is √(L/g). Mass can't appear.
- *So T ∝ √(L/g).* The full answer is 2π√(L/g).
> The same trick estimated the first atomic bomb's yield

story 5 | G. I. Taylor worked out a classified bomb yield from public photos
- Photos of the 1945 Trinity fireball, with a scale bar and timestamps, were released after the war.
- Taylor argued the blast radius could only depend on energy E, time t and air density ρ: R ∝ (Et²/ρ)^(1/5).
- His 1950 estimate of about 17 kilotons was close to the still-secret official figure of about 20.
> Fermi had estimated it at the test itself, by dropping scraps of paper

quiz 3 | What are the SI base units of force (the newton)?
* kg·m/s²
o kg·m/s
o kg·m²/s²
o m/s²
= F = ma: kilograms times metres per second squared.
= kg·m²/s² is energy (joules).
> Fermi problems: estimate anything to within a factor of 10

real 3 | How many piano tuners in Chicago? A Fermi estimate
- ~3 million people, ~1 piano per 20 households of ~2 people: ~75,000 pianos.
- Tuned once a year; a tuner does ~4 a day, ~250 days a year: ~1,000 a year each.
- *Roughly 75 tuners.* Each step is rough, but errors partly cancel.
> Scientific notation keeps the zeros under control

quiz 3 | Roughly how many seconds in a year?
* 3 × 10⁷
o 3 × 10⁵
o 10⁹
o 8.6 × 10⁴
= 365 × 24 × 3600 ≈ 3.15 × 10⁷. "π × 10⁷" is a famous physicist's shortcut.
= 8.6 × 10⁴ is seconds in a day.
> Next: motion in one dimension

## p1-1d | Motion in a line | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-units
explain 2 | The SUVAT equations (constant acceleration only)
- v = u + at
- s = ut + ½at²
- v² = u² + 2as
- s = ½(u + v)t
? Which equation links v, u, a and s without t? :: v² = u² + 2as
> They all come from integrating a constant acceleration twice

quiz 3 | A car brakes from 30 m/s at 6 m/s². Stopping distance?
* 75 m
o 150 m
o 30 m
o 5 m
= v² = u² + 2as: 0 = 900 − 12s, s = 75 m.
> Double the speed, quadruple the stopping distance

real 4 | Why speeding is so much more dangerous than it feels
- Braking distance ∝ v². 40 mph needs four times the braking distance of 20 mph.
- Kinetic energy ∝ v² too: crashing at 40 delivers four times the energy.
- *Small speed increases have outsized consequences.*
> Graphs tell the same story

explain 2 | Reading motion graphs
- Position–time: slope is velocity.
- Velocity–time: slope is acceleration, *area under it is displacement*.
- Acceleration–time: area is change in velocity.
> Free fall is the classic constant acceleration

quiz 3 | Drop a stone off a 45 m cliff (g = 10 m/s²). Time to hit the ground?
* 3 s
o 4.5 s
o 9 s
o 2 s
= 45 = ½ × 10 × t², so t² = 9.
> Throw it upward and the time up equals the time down (without air)

predict 3 | You throw a ball straight up. At the very top, what is its acceleration?
* 9.8 m/s² downward
o Zero
o It depends on how hard you threw it
= Velocity is momentarily zero. Acceleration never is: gravity keeps pulling.
= This is one of the most common exam mistakes.
> Next: two dimensions

## p1-proj | Projectiles | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-1d
explain 2 | Projectiles: two independent motions
- Horizontal: constant velocity (no air resistance).
- Vertical: constant acceleration g.
- They share only the time of flight.
> Drop a bullet and fire one: which lands first?

predict 4 | A bullet is fired horizontally and another is dropped from the same height at the same moment. Which hits the ground first?
* Same time
o The dropped one
o The fired one
= Vertical motion is identical for both. Horizontal speed doesn't change the fall.
= (On a flat Earth with no air. The real bullet travels far enough for Earth's curvature to matter slightly.)
> Time of flight depends only on vertical motion

quiz 3 | A ball is launched at 20 m/s at 30° above flat ground. Time in the air (g = 10)?
* 2 s
o 4 s
o 1 s
o 3.5 s
= Vertical speed 20 sin 30° = 10 m/s. Up for 1 s, down for 1 s.
> Range peaks at 45°, but only without air

real 3 | Why shot putters release below 45°
- They release from shoulder height, above the landing point. That shifts the best angle below 45°.
- Humans can also push harder at lower angles.
- *Elite shot putters release around 35–40°.*
> Air resistance changes everything at high speed

explain 4 | Air resistance at speed goes as v²
- Drag force ≈ ½ρC_dAv².
- Terminal velocity when drag equals weight: a skydiver tops out around 55 m/s belly-down.
- With drag, trajectories aren't parabolas: they come down steeper than they went up.
> Next: Newton's laws

## p1-nl | Newton's laws workout | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-proj
explain 2 | Newton's three laws
- 1. No net force: velocity stays constant (including zero).
- 2. F_net = ma.
- 3. Forces come in equal and opposite pairs acting on different objects.
> The first law was revolutionary

story 4 | Aristotle thought motion needs a constant push
- On Earth everything stops, so Aristotle concluded moving things need continuous force.
- Galileo imagined ramps without friction: a ball would roll forever.
- *Friction hid the true law for 2,000 years.*
> Draw the free-body diagram first, always

quiz 3 | A 2 kg box is pulled with 10 N on a frictionless floor. Acceleration?
* 5 m/s²
o 20 m/s²
o 0.2 m/s²
o 10 m/s²
= a = F/m = 10/2.
> Connected bodies: Atwood's machine

quiz 4 | Atwood machine: 3 kg and 5 kg over a frictionless pulley. Acceleration? (g = 10)
* 2.5 m/s²
o 5 m/s²
o 10 m/s²
o 1.25 m/s²
= Net force (5 − 3) × 10 = 20 N. Total mass 8 kg. a = 2.5 m/s².
> Tension isn't simply the weight

predict 4 | In that Atwood machine, is the string tension more or less than 30 N (the light mass's weight)?
* More: 37.5 N
o Less
o Exactly 30 N
= The 3 kg mass accelerates upward: T − 30 = 3 × 2.5, so T = 37.5 N.
> Normal force isn't always mg either

myth 3 F | The normal force on an object always equals its weight.
= On a slope it's mg cos θ. In an accelerating lift it's m(g + a).
= Push down on a book and the normal force grows. It's whatever stops you sinking into the surface.
> Next: friction and slopes

## p1-fric | Friction and inclines | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-nl
explain 3 | Static vs kinetic friction
- Static friction adjusts up to a maximum μ_s N. Kinetic friction is μ_k N.
- μ_s > μ_k: it's harder to start something sliding than to keep it going.
- *That's why ABS brakes work: a rolling tyre uses static friction, a skidding one uses weaker kinetic friction.*
> On a slope, gravity splits into two parts

explain 3 | Forces on an incline
- Along the slope: mg sin θ. Into the slope: mg cos θ.
- Normal force N = mg cos θ.
- A block starts sliding when tan θ > μ_s.
? Angle at which a block starts to slide on an incline? :: When tan θ = μ_s
> Measure μ with a protractor

quiz 3 | A block just starts to slide when a ramp reaches 30°. μ_s = ?
* tan 30° ≈ 0.58
o sin 30° = 0.5
o cos 30° ≈ 0.87
o 0.3
= mg sin θ = μ_s mg cos θ gives μ_s = tan θ.
> Friction is why you can walk at all

predict 3 | When you walk forward, which way does friction on your foot point?
* Forward
o Backward
o There's no friction while walking
= Your foot pushes the ground backward; friction pushes you forward.
= On ice, there's too little of it and your foot slides back.
> Next: going round in circles

## p1-circ | Circular motion | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-fric
explain 3 | Moving in a circle means accelerating inward
- Even at constant speed, direction changes, so there's acceleration v²/r toward the centre.
- Something must provide that force: tension, friction, gravity, normal force.
- *Centripetal isn't a new force.* It's a job one of the real forces does.
? Centripetal acceleration? :: v²/r toward the centre
> Centrifugal force is what it feels like from inside

myth 4 F | When you swing a ball on a string and let go, it flies straight outward from the centre.
= It flies off along the tangent, in the direction it was moving at that instant.
= Nothing was pushing it outward. The string was pulling it in.
> Banked tracks let cars turn without friction

quiz 4 | A car takes a 50 m radius bend at 20 m/s. What friction coefficient is needed on a flat road? (g = 10)
* At least 0.8
o At least 0.4
o At least 0.2
o At least 1.6
= v²/r = 400/50 = 8 m/s². μg ≥ 8 so μ ≥ 0.8.
> Loops need minimum speed at the top

quiz 3 | Minimum speed at the top of a vertical loop of radius r?
* √(gr)
o √(2gr)
o gr
o 0
= At the top, gravity alone can supply mv²/r when v² = gr.
= Any slower and you fall off.
> Next: work and energy

## p1-we | Work and energy | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-circ
explain 2 | Work: force times distance along the force
- W = F·d = Fd cos θ.
- Carrying a bag horizontally at constant speed: the gravity and lift forces do *zero* work.
- Work-energy theorem: net work = change in kinetic energy.
> Energy is what's conserved when forces are conservative

quiz 3 | A 2 kg ball drops 5 m. Speed at the bottom? (g = 10, no air)
* 10 m/s
o 50 m/s
o 5 m/s
o 100 m/s
= mgh = ½mv²: v = √(2gh) = √100.
= Mass cancels.
> Springs store energy as ½kx²

quiz 3 | A spring (k = 200 N/m) is compressed 0.1 m. Stored energy?
* 1 J
o 10 J
o 20 J
o 2 J
= ½kx² = ½ × 200 × 0.01.
> Power is energy per second

real 3 | How much power do you produce climbing stairs?
- 70 kg climbing 3 m of stairs in 3 s: mgh/t ≈ 70 × 10 × 3 / 3 = 700 W.
- That's nearly one horsepower (746 W), for a few seconds.
- *A fit cyclist sustains about 250–400 W for an hour.*
> Energy is never destroyed, just harder to use

explain 3 | Where the "lost" energy goes
- A sliding block stops. Its kinetic energy became heat in the surfaces.
- Total energy is conserved. Mechanical energy isn't, when friction acts.
- *"Losing" energy really means turning it into forms you can't easily use.*
> Next: momentum

## p1-mom | Momentum and collisions | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-we
explain 2 | Momentum is conserved in every collision
- p = mv. With no external force, total momentum stays constant.
- Energy may or may not be conserved; momentum always is.
- Elastic: kinetic energy conserved too. Inelastic: some becomes heat and sound.
> Perfectly inelastic: they stick together

quiz 3 | A 1,000 kg car at 20 m/s hits a stationary 1,000 kg car and they lock together. Speed after?
* 10 m/s
o 20 m/s
o 5 m/s
o 0
= 1000 × 20 = 2000 × v.
= Half the kinetic energy turns into crumpled metal and heat.
> Impulse explains crumple zones and airbags

real 4 | Airbags work by making the stop take longer
- Impulse: FΔt = Δp. The change in momentum is fixed by your speed.
- Stretch the stopping time from 0.01 s to 0.1 s and the average force drops tenfold.
- *Crumple zones, bent knees on landing, catching an egg gently: same physics.*
> Elastic collisions between equal masses swap velocities

predict 3 | A snooker ball hits an identical stationary ball head-on (elastic). What happens?
* The first stops dead, the second moves off at the original speed
o Both move forward at half speed
o The first bounces back
= Equal masses in a head-on elastic collision swap velocities.
> Glancing collisions between equal masses leave at 90°

explain 4 | Equal-mass elastic glancing collisions split at 90°
- Momentum: v = v₁ + v₂ (vectors). Energy: v² = v₁² + v₂².
- Pythagoras means v₁ ⊥ v₂.
- *Snooker players use the 90° rule to judge where the cue ball goes.*
> Rockets use momentum conservation too
